#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Update a Sculpt OS test guest (make-sculpt.sh) the way Sculpt updates itself.

Usage: sculpt-update [--check | --try] [--image VERSION] [--cache DIR] [--offline]
                     [--publish] WORK_DIR
  e.g. sculpt-update --check vms/sculpt2610
       sculpt-update vms/sculpt2610

Sculpt has no sshd and no package manager vm-update could drive: it updates
itself in its Leitzentrale, System > Update.  There, for a depot user
(genodelabs), "Check for updates" fetches the user's image index, the list of
the system images Genode Labs publishes; picking an image downloads it, and
"Install" copies it into /boot, which the next boot runs.  What that leaves
on the GENODE* partition, from Sculpt 26.10's own code (Genode's
sculpt-26.10 tag: repos/gems/src/app/sculpt_manager/main.cc
install_boot_image and view/software_update_widget.h, the
depot_download_manager's gen_fetchurl.cc and gen_extract.cc, fs_tool's
copy-all-files):

  /public/genodelabs/image/index.xz, index.xz.sig
                    the image index as downloaded (fetchurl), kept
  /depot/genodelabs/image/index
                    the index, unpacked (extract name="index" into
                    image/extract/, then renamed over the old one)
  /public/genodelabs/image/sculpt-pc-VERSION.tar.xz, .tar.xz.sig
                    the image as downloaded, kept: nothing removes /public's
                    downloads
  /depot/genodelabs/image/sculpt-pc-VERSION/
                    the archive unpacked (bender, image-hw.elf.gz, boot.png,
                    font.pf2, grub/grub.cfg): extracted into image/extract/
                    and renamed into place
  /boot/*, /boot/grub/*
                    install: every regular file of that folder copied into
                    /boot over what is there (the same file, truncated and
                    written anew: its inode, owner and mode stay), and on the
                    pc board those of its grub/ into /boot/grub.  Nothing else
                    in /boot is removed or touched: from a 26.04 image (NOVA:
                    image.elf.gz and hypervisor) to a 26.10 one (base-hw:
                    image-hw.elf.gz), the old two stay, and the new grub.cfg
                    no longer boots them.

Nothing under /config changes: the update and install keep no state of
their own (the dialog's "reboot to activate" is in memory only), and the
installed image says what it is itself: its build_info ROM, inside the
image grub.cfg boots (/boot/image-hw.elf.gz on 26.10, /boot/image.elf.gz on
26.04: genode_version sculpt-26.10, date 2026-10-30, board pc, ...), is what
Sculpt's own dialog reads.  sculpt-update reads the same.

sculpt-update writes exactly those files and folders, with Sculpt's
contents, names and layout, but it can't be Sculpt's file system driver:
  - new files and folders get what every inode Sculpt wrote on its first
    boot has (debugfs on a make-sculpt.sh guest): owner 0:0, mode 0777, no
    i_extra_isize, a, c and mtime 0 for folders, and mtime 2018-01-01
    00:01:00 UTC with a and ctime 0 for files.  A file /boot already had
    keeps its owner, mode, atime and ctime, and takes that mtime.  Inferred
    from what the first boot left, not from Sculpt's driver itself;
  - inode numbers and block placement are debugfs's (Sculpt's driver spreads
    new folders over the block groups; a /boot file rewritten here is freed
    and written again, and usually gets its old inode back);
  - any block the update frees is zeroed where a whole qcow2 cluster of
    them is free, so the image doesn't keep it (Sculpt leaves free blocks
    as they are; nothing reads them).  In practice debugfs writes a /boot
    file anew into the blocks it just freed, so little is freed, and each
    update adds about its archive's size three times to the image (the
    download kept in /public, the depot copy, /boot): some 100 MiB, as on
    Sculpt itself.

It does all that offline, on a qcow2 overlay over the guest's image, which
is only read:
  1. a real update first checks, as vm-update's step 1, that nothing uses the
     image (a QEMU program, a qcow2 overlay on top of it, a merge left
     undone) and that it is still what vms/GUEST/image-state says; a guest
     with no image-state gets one there, generation and all, as vm-update
     starts one (so a no-op run on a state-less guest starts its state).  It
     holds vm-update's fleet lock (vms/.vm-update.fleet.lock) until it is
     done, so no vm-store publish or vm-update round runs meanwhile;
  2. fetches the image index and checks its signature (gpgv with only the
     Genode Labs depot key, WORK_DIR/genodelabs.pub, fingerprint pinned
     below, as make-sculpt.sh checks the release's image), reads the
     installed image's build_info from the disk, and picks the newest pc
     image of the guest's own release (the release its name says:
     sculpt2610 is 26.10, so 26.10.1 and later point releases, never 27.04:
     a new release is a new guest), or --image's.  Nothing newer: nothing
     more is done;
  3. downloads it (sculpt-pc-VERSION.tar.xz and its .sig, kept in the
     cache) and checks it with the same key, and that the image inside (the one
     its grub.cfg boots) is that version for the pc board;
  4. makes a raw copy of the disk in the scratch folder (the RAM disk:
     sparse, about what the disk holds), writes Sculpt's files there with
     debugfs -w, checks them (each file read back against its source, and
     e2fsck -fn), and then writes into the overlay only the 64 KiB clusters
     that differ from the image, through a FUSE export of the overlay
     (qemu-storage-daemon): the overlay holds the update and nothing else.
     qemu-img compare then checks that the overlay on the image reads as the
     raw copy, and the image's SHA-256 that it wasn't written;
  5. boots the overlay with sculpt-check.sh --disk, which also checks that
     the image grub.cfg boots is the new one.  --try stops here and throws
     the overlay away;
  6. merges it into the image as vm-update's step 5 does on one host (its
     own code: qemu-img commit, the "fresh-install" snapshot retaken,
     qcow2-punch.py), and records the new generation in image-state (the
     old one and the overlay's SHA-256, chained) and image-stat.json, and
     what is installed in WORK_DIR/sculpt-image.json;
  7. with --publish, runs `vm-store publish GUEST` (Chopin: the store's
     only publisher).  Without it, it says to.  Then each cache host takes
     the new version with `vm-store link GUEST`.  No image is copied host to
     host by hand.

It also writes WORK_DIR/no-ssh (as make-sculpt.sh does), which makes
vm-update and compare.py refuse the guest at once.  Never pass a Sculpt
guest to vm-update.

Real updates and starts run on the fleet's first host only (Chopin, for the
real vms/), or in a tree of their own (BKO_WORK).  Like vm-update, it takes
the guest's vms/GUEST/.vm-update.lock, and logs to vms/GUEST/update.log.

Options:
  --check        only say what is installed and what the index offers, and
                 exit: 0 the newest image of the guest's release is
                 installed, 10 a newer one of its release is there (what a
                 plain sculpt-update installs), 11 none of its release but a
                 newer release is out (a new guest's job; 10 wins when both
                 hold, and the text says both), 1 an error.  It downloads the
                 index into a scratch folder and writes nothing else (not
                 even update.log), so it runs on a cache host's vm-store
                 folder too
  --try          steps 1-5 (no state started, no lock), then throw it away
  --image VERSION
                 install the pc image VERSION (YYYY-MM-DD) whether or not it
                 is newer or of the guest's release (it says so when not); it
                 need not be in the index (the index lists only the latest
                 few), only on depot.genode.org
  --cache DIR    downloads go in DIR/genodelabs/image/ (default
                 $SCULPT_UPDATE_CACHE, else /bkoscratch/sculpt-update, else
                 ~/.cache/sculpt-update).  The images are kept; the index is
                 fetched anew each run
  --offline      use only what the cache holds, the index too (tests)
  --publish      after a merge or a start, run vm-store publish GUEST

Environment: BKO_WORK (the tree whose vms/ holds the guest; default the
folder above WORK_DIR's vms/), SCULPT_UPDATE_SCRATCH (the scratch folder's
parent; default /mnt/bko-ram if it is there, else TMPDIR), WEAR_TAG.  Run
heavy work under `cmp-run.sh --reserve 2 4 ...` (the raw copy and the boot
check's VM: about 2 GiB of RAM disk and 4 GiB of memory).

Needs: python3, qemu-img, qemu-storage-daemon and fusermount3 (/dev/fuse), e2fsprogs
(debugfs, e2fsck, dumpe2fs), gpg and gpgv, curl, and what sculpt-check.sh
needs.
"""

